##  What does `@Singleton` mean in Spring Boot?

### 🔹 Layman Analogy:

Imagine a **coffee machine (singleton)** in an office.

* 100 employees (requests) use it.
* Only **1 coffee machine** exists.
* Everyone shares it.
  They don’t create a new machine per coffee!

---

### Technical Explanation:

Spring Boot (using Spring container) **creates one instance** of:

* `@Controller`
* `@Service`
* `@Repository`
  …and keeps it in the **ApplicationContext**.

When **1000 users** hit your API:

* All those requests are **served by the same singleton beans**
* Local data is stored per thread, not inside the singleton itself

So YES:

> **N requests (1000, 10k, whatever) all use the same 1 singleton bean**

---

##  Important Clarification

###  Don’t put request-specific data in singleton fields:

```java
@Service
public class BadService {
    private String username; // ❌ BAD: shared across all threads
}
```

This creates race conditions — data from one request leaks into another.

---

###  Do this instead (safe):

```java
@Service
public class GoodService {
    public String greet(String username) {
        return "Hello " + username;
    }
}
```

* All data passed in **method arguments**
* No shared mutable fields
* Fully **thread-safe**

---

##  Summary Table

| Component       | Scope       | How many instances? | Thread-safe?          |
| --------------- | ----------- | ------------------- | --------------------- |
| `@Controller`   | Singleton   | 1 shared            | Yes, if stateless     |
| `@Service`      | Singleton   | 1 shared            | Yes, if stateless     |
| `@Repository`   | Singleton   | 1 shared            | Yes                   |
| Request DTO     | Per request | 1 per request       | No sharing            |
| Local variables | Per request | Reside in thread    | Thread-specific stack |




